  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an integer NNN.\n\nConstruct a permutation P=[P1,P2,...,PN]P = [P_1, P_2, ..., P_N]P=[P1​,P2​,...,PN​] such that there does not exist iii and jjj (1≤i≤j≤N1 \\le i \\le j \\le N1≤i≤j≤N) such that (Pi+Pi+1+...+Pj)mod  (N+1)=0(P_i + P_{i + 1} + ... + P_j) \\mod (N + 1) = 0(Pi​+Pi+1​+...+Pj​)mod(N+1)=0. Informally, all subarray sums should not be divisible by (N+1)(N + 1)(N+1).\n\nIf it is not possible, print −1-1−1 instead.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first and only line of each test case contains a single integer NNN.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line :\n\n\n\nNNN space separated integers representing the permutation PPP if a valid permutation exists.\n−1-1−1 if no valid permutation exists.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤2001 \\le T \\le 2001≤T≤200",
      "1≤N≤1031 \\le N \\le 10^31≤N≤103"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n1\n2\n3\n4",
        "output": "1\n-1\n1 2 3\n-1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 2 : It can be proven that no permutation is valid. For example, consider P=[1,2]P = [1, 2]P=[1,2], then the subarray [1,2][1, 2][1,2] has a sum of 333 which is divisible by (N+1)=3(N + 1) = 3(N+1)=3.\nTest case 3 : It can be proven that all subarrays are not divisible by N+1N + 1N+1 for the permutation P=[1,2,3]P = [1, 2, 3]P=[1,2,3]. For example, the subarray [1,2][1, 2][1,2] has a sum of 333 which is not divisible by N+1=4N + 1 = 4N+1=4."
      }
    ]
  },
